1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,483
India's biggest weather network already exists.

2
00:00:02,483 --> 00:00:08,690
It has lakhs of stations, it covers every district, and it's switched on right now.

3
00:00:08,690 --> 00:00:11,586
Nobody noticed — because we call it Jio.

4
00:00:12,286 --> 00:00:16,010
Weather in India is hyperlocal, but our data isn't.

5
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:19,734
About forty Doppler radars watch 3.3 million square kilometres.

6
00:00:19,734 --> 00:00:24,700
When a cloudburst hits one ward, the people who lose the most —

7
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:28,838
farmers, delivery riders, construction workers, street vendors — lose it alone.

8
00:00:28,838 --> 00:00:33,390
Heat alone could cost India thirty-four million full-time jobs by 2030.

9
00:00:33,390 --> 00:00:36,700
And when insurance does exist, claims take months.

10
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:38,641
Here's the insight.

11
00:00:38,641 --> 00:00:41,538
Rain weakens the radio links between towers —

12
00:00:41,538 --> 00:00:47,331
and how much it weakens tells you how hard it's raining, minute by minute.

13
00:00:47,331 --> 00:00:51,055
Smartphone batteries, averaged across a city, track air temperature.

14
00:00:51,055 --> 00:00:56,021
So every tower is a rain gauge. Every phone is a thermometer.

15
00:00:56,021 --> 00:00:59,331
We don't build sensors. We become the sensor.

16
00:01:00,031 --> 00:01:02,928
Does it actually work? We built it.

17
00:01:02,928 --> 00:01:07,893
We ran our prototype on 500 real microwave links for eleven days —

18
00:01:07,893 --> 00:01:10,790
and checked it against national weather radar.

19
00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:15,341
Network-wide, our rain readings match radar with a correlation of 0.95.

20
00:01:15,341 --> 00:01:21,134
Our payout rule made the same decision as radar ninety-five percent of the time.

21
00:01:21,834 --> 00:01:22,662
Meet JioMausam —

22
00:01:22,662 --> 00:01:26,386
a live, street-level weather map built from network data.

23
00:01:26,386 --> 00:01:28,869
On top of it: Mausam Kavach —

24
00:01:28,869 --> 00:01:33,834
parametric micro-insurance you add to your recharge for twenty-nine rupees a month.

25
00:01:33,834 --> 00:01:40,455
When the network measures 45 degrees in your area, or rain heavy enough to stop work,

26
00:01:40,455 --> 00:01:44,179
the payout lands in your JioPay wallet within hours.

27
00:01:44,179 --> 00:01:47,076
No claim forms. No surveyor. No waiting.

28
00:01:47,776 --> 00:01:49,845
The business has three layers.

29
00:01:49,845 --> 00:01:55,638
One: Kavach premiums — one percent of Jio's 500 million users is five million policies —

30
00:01:55,638 --> 00:01:58,121
around 170 crore rupees a year.

31
00:01:58,121 --> 00:02:03,914
Two: B2B — gig platforms buy cover for riders; insurers and states buy hyperlocal data.

32
00:02:03,914 --> 00:02:05,983
Three: Reliance is customer zero —

33
00:02:05,983 --> 00:02:12,190
JioMart forecasts rainy-day demand, New Energy forecasts solar output, and Jio predicts its own rain-fade.

34
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:14,131
Why only Reliance?

35
00:02:14,131 --> 00:02:16,614
A startup can't own the towers.

36
00:02:16,614 --> 00:02:19,510
An insurer can't reach 500 million people.

37
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:24,890
No other telecom has a payments app, an insurance arm, a retail chain

38
00:02:24,890 --> 00:02:28,200
and a giga-scale energy business under one roof.

39
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:29,855
Reliance has every layer.

40
00:02:30,555 --> 00:02:36,348
We start with a ninety-day pilot in Vidarbha and Ahmedabad, validated against IMD gauges.

41
00:02:36,348 --> 00:02:40,072
Then an IRDAI sandbox product. Then a national map.

42
00:02:40,072 --> 00:02:44,624
India won't adapt to climate change only by building new infrastructure.

43
00:02:44,624 --> 00:02:48,762
It'll adapt by listening to the infrastructure it already has.

44
00:02:48,762 --> 00:02:51,245
This is JioMausam. Different by design.
